This program REPLACES the runtime's write/exit provider (#1367 SEAM 3), so it
never reaches `statsReport` and emits no `[bit-gc] collections=...` line however
`BIT_GC_STATS` is set. Its own `_exit` through libSystem is the thing under test:
the harness's aggregate-line requirement is a property of the standard boot path,
and this program deliberately is not on it.

Declared rather than tolerated, and the distinction matters. A missing stats line
is normally FATAL, because it is what a broken environment or a broken capture
looks like — and treating it as "this build does not report the field" would turn
the collection oracle off for the whole corpus in one line with no error text
anywhere. This file says the absence is a property of THIS program, and the cost
is stated: the collection and abandonment oracles do not apply here. The stdout
comparison against `rootiodarwin.expected` still does, under both policies.

Its Linux twin `../rootiolinux` has the same shape (§11.8 `syscall` rather than
§11.7 `extern function`) and now has the same file. It was deliberately NOT added
here at the same time: the case had never been observed failing on a host that
runs it, and a marker written from inference rather than measurement is exactly
the kind that outlives its reason. `scripts/arm64gate.sh` then reported it on
real aarch64-linux (part of #1875), which is the measurement, and the twin
records it.
